A||||12||KitKar||Longitudinal V-8||||1||
Z||000000||KitKar||08-22-2000||12:55 PM||||I thought I would bring up the engine conversion discussion/debate again.<BR>Has anyone done the longitudinal V-8 conversion? I heard it is supposed to be much cheaper than the Archie or Zumalt kits. I heard $500 - $1,000 plus engine. I figure I could get an inexpensive carbed one (If it will pass emissions) for now. Then later I could put in a Corvette engine or something. That way it would only cost $1,000 - $1,500 in theory. Any thoughts. The only problem is I really don't like the idea of converting to an auto.<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||baldlobo||08-22-2000||01:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KitKar:<BR><B>I thought I would bring up the engine conversion discussion/debate again.<BR>Has anyone done the longitudinal V-8 conversion? I heard it is supposed to be much cheaper than the Archie or Zumalt kits. I heard $500 - $1,000 plus engine. I figure I could get an inexpensive carbed one (If it will pass emissions) for now. Then later I could put in a Corvette engine or something. That way it would only cost $1,000 - $1,500 in theory. Any thoughts. The only problem is I really don't like the idea of converting to an auto.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've got an Idea, attach the V-8 to a old porsche manual 5-speed or maybe a VW Trans. with a conversion kit for the trans., you would probably have to do some other things to the rear end.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KitKar||08-22-2000||03:12 PM||||That doesn't sound like a bad idea.<BR>Does anyone have any knowledge on this? Could this work? I don't know any thing about Porches. What else would I need? What kind of axles etc.?<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||baldlobo||08-22-2000||04:42 PM||||you would either have to get someone to make you up a pair of one of a kinds or if the porsche shafts are long enough you could probably change out the rear pieces in between the wheels and the drive shafts for the porsche.<P>PS- <A HREF="http://www.renegadehybirds.com" TARGET=_blank>www.renegadehybirds.com</A>  has V-8 conversion packages<p>[This message has been edited by baldlobo (edited 08-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||08-22-2000||07:51 PM||||S.J. Wynman of Associated Auto, Inc  201 646 1593  made this longitudinal V8.  He has written a manual on how to do the conversion. I don't know the cost.  The engine cradle was specially fabricated.  I doubt that is is any less expensive than Archies or Zumwalts, since it is a one of a kind design.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Wyman.jpg"> <P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||KitKar||08-23-2000||12:35 AM||||Actually, it is Joe Wynmans conversion I was talking about. I did get the manual. I guess the cradle is not that big a deal to have a welder do. Joe says it should be $500 - $1,000 plus engine. I just wanted to see if anyone else who did that conversion had any comments on it.<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||08-23-2000||02:53 AM||||In the February 2000 issue of High Performance Pontiac there is an article with pictures about a 455 cu inch engine in a Fiero mounted longitudally.  An '81 Buick Riviera transmission was used with a custom "rear transverse spring style" (like a late model Corvette) suspension.  Of course (as also seen in the picture above) you lose a large portion of the trunk space to engine.<P>Seems like a more costly path than the transverse mounted V8, but I don't have a clue as to how one would go about the whole swap.<P>Porsche transaxles would be great, but they are very expensive.  I have also heard of the FWD Toronado transmission being used for longitudally mounting a V8 into a Fiero.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Xtreme Fiero's||08-23-2000||10:25 AM||||Rayne Motorsports makes a Tubular Cradle that has Ferrari like rear coil overs, it take a small block chevy engine and a porshe trans. I think is costs about $2200 with A-arms & and coil over But the small block i belive just bolts in and all you do to the fiero is cut out the trunk and bolt up the cradle. Does any one know more about this thing? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DL10||08-23-2000||11:55 AM||||I think the total cost would be higher, and you do lose all trunk space. There would be more weigth behind the rear wheels which I think would affect handling. But it does look great....... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||08-23-2000||12:21 PM||||remember me saying a few months back some guy told me he could put in a v8 for $500 plus the engine? i didn't much believe him. maybe this was what he was talking about. still haven't run across him again. i may have to start looking more seriously for him. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||05:43 PM||||Here's my 2 cents.  Some of the Porsche transaxles are <B>VERY</B> expensive.  The 930 version is bulletproof, but about $8000.<P>One of the reasons the Fiero handles so well is that it's a <B>mid</B> engined car.  Putting the motor the wrong way will make it a <B>rear</B> engined car.  Porsche 911s are tail happy for a reason.  Ever seen a 911 with the Turbo-look package?  The rear wheels must be 12 feet wide.<P>You'll lose your trunk.  Where else are you gonna store stuff?  In the back seat?<P>I'll bet you spend more for the V8 this way.  And you don't have any recourse if you screw it up.  Personal feelings aside, I've never heard anything bad about Archies quality of work and I would imagine that, if something <I>did</I> go wrong, he'd be there in a heartbeat to fix it.<P>I'd say to go with the transverse V8.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||08-23-2000||09:59 PM||||I've done a 350TPI/TH325-4L swap. If you can handle a cutting torch and a welder, it is far cheaper than a transverse V8 swap. Mine was well under $500+engine, but I did 99% of the work myself. I started a write-up on the subject, but never really finished. <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html</A> <P>Hugh, where are you? Do you mind if I use that picture you sent me on my website?<P>The chevy/porsch setup has to be by far the most expensive. you need to first acquire the trans($$$$) and an adapter (more$$$$) and a custom cradle ($$$$), etc.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||7
Z||000012||hugh||08-23-2000||10:07 PM||||That's fine with me.Thank you for asking.||||reg||1
